re  the brightly colored rocks  we even saw a few bats  then we climbed another 300 meters to  reach our final destination  a cave adorned with ancient rock  paintings created by some of the  region's  original inhabitants  vicente rodriguez says the paintings  have many similarities with  other famous archaeological sites like  chauvet cave in france  he says they're among the oldest in the  world  they were painted in about 10 000 bc  by tribes who moved around this area  while many of the paintings have faded  over the centuries  they still possess invaluable cultural  significance  they're an expression of the  spirituality that shaped the lives of  mexico's first  peoples human figures can be seen among  the images of dogs and other animals  as well as abstract shapes and patterns  the paints that were used were all  natural for example  coal mud or even animal fat  60 pigments have been found in the  district of tlatsko 39 in la cienega  8 in la palma 12 in rosario  and 2 in mesas the mexican national  institute of anthropology and history  has catalogued  and analyzed the rock paintings  it's calling on mexican authorities to  protect the paintings which have been  damaged over time  the cave's owner ricardo martinez plans  to set up an  information stand so that the public  will be able to visit these caves  in the future  digital photography is changing the way  people preserve their memories  and it's also changing how much they're  willing to pay for those memories  in hanoi freelance photographers are  finding many people  no longer want to make an appointment  and pose for portraits  when they could just snap selfies with  their smartphone  ho tv in vietnam reports on a dying  trade  every weekend 80-year-old freelance  photographer che winn  rides to the capitol's botanical gardens  to work  some 15 years ago he would not have been  alone  nowadays he says it's almost impossible  to make a living from freelance  photography  as customers willing to pay for  professional portraits are dwindling  in the past there were many freelance  photographers here  at that time photographers could make  enough money  to even buy a house in hanoi  these days people have their own cameras  they're light and easy to use  the digital photography revolution has  made hanoi's freelance photographers  almost obsolete  i have a smartphone and a digital camera  so it's easy and convenient to take  pictures of my friends and family  hiring a photographer is very time  consuming because you have to wait for  them to print the photos  digital photography has allowed people  to gain better knowledge of the craft  and help them develop more discerning  tastes  a freelance photographer can't keep up  with us  and besides the quality of freelance  photographers varies quite a bit  has spent around 20 years taking photos  at honkem lake  he used to take hundreds of pictures for  customers now he's lucky if he gets a  dozen orders  he also runs a photography club with  about 25 members  but there used to be twice as many  we advise younger photographers to see  freelance photography only as a way of  making a bit of extra money  on top of a full-time job  most don't know what to do  with so many amateur photographers  around these days hanoi's freelance  street photographers fear they will soon  be a thing of the past  that's all for this edition of world  stories from all of us here in berlin  and our partner stations around the  world thanks for tuning in  to watch this episode and others head to  our website the address there  d w dot d e world stories before we go  check out this week's picks of the world  enjoy and see you next time  so  you

